<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arad, Romania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arad is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on the Mureș river, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> until 1920. After the defeat of the Hungarian revolution of 1848-1849, thirteen Hungarian generals were executed there by the Austrians on 6 October 1849. These Martyrs of Arad are commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as national heroes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arad-romania.jpg" alt="Arad, Romania" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nick Savchenko from Kiev, Ukraine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arad_(30130671351).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement on the northern bank of the Mureș began in the 5th millennium BCE. Roman troops conquered the Dacian settlement in what is now the Micălaca district between 101 and 102, and in the Second Dacian War Trajan took the land north of the Mureș. The name of the city comes from its first ispán, or count, Arad. The area became part of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> at the end of the 9th century, and the town is first mentioned in the 11th century.</p><p>An assembly of the realm in 1131 was followed by the massacre of 68 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> blamed for the blinding of King Béla II. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took the region in 1551 and held it until the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlowitz</a> of 1699, and Austrian forces captured the town in 1687. A new fortress was built between 1763 and 1783, and in 1834 Emperor Francis I declared Arad a free royal town. In the Revolution of 1848 it became a Hungarian headquarters, and from Arad Lajos Kossuth issued his proclamation of 11 August 1849 and handed supreme power to Artúr Görgey.</p><p>After Görgey's surrender at Világos, the fortress was recaptured, and on 6 October 1849 Julius Jacob von Haynau had thirteen rebel generals executed there. Arad has since been called the Hungarian Golgotha. In 1910 its 63,166 inhabitants were 73 per cent Hungarians and 16.2 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>. In 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Arad is the capital of Arad County, on the Mureș, and a transport hub. Its growth in the later 19th and early 20th centuries produced its main buildings, among them the neoclassical Ioan Slavici Theatre of 1874 and the Neo-Gothic Red Church of 1906. The city had one of the first music conservatories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, set up in 1833, and the first car factory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Preparandia, founded in 1812, was the first Romanian teacher-training school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, and the Hirschl Theatre of 1817 is the oldest stone theatre in Romania. A monument to the thirteen martyrs stands in one of the public squares.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arad, Romania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade routes from Arabia through the Negev brought incense and spices, and fortresses such as <b>Arad</b> guarded them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Thirteen generals were executed at <b>Arad</b> in October, and Kossuth went into exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-state-1849">Hungarian State (1849)</a></li></ul></section>
